  abstract = {Acropolis is a social computing platform especially designed to engage citizens in building and sharing their own narratives about complex or long-term news stories. The first two design cycles of the platform have shown, by positive evidence, that this goal has been achieved, but in the previous papers no analysis was conducted regarding the social behavior of the participants in relation to consuming or being exposed to different viewpoints of their own. Several studies suggest that people crave more for reinforcement of opinion than they reject exposure from different points of view, and that from the standpoint of strengthening democratic discourse, hearing the other side is crucial. A key goal of this paper is to evaluate the extent to which the participants of the latest experiment in the aforementioned platform followed this behavior. Besides that, we aim to investigate whether the design of the platform favored the goal of encouraging the consumption of diverse sources and opinions, with an eye towards the next design cycles.},
